  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Defining Server Logic**\n\nI\'ve decided to tackle the `server.ts` file now. First, I need to outline its core responsibilities. It will handle API routes, starting with a signup route (`POST /api/auth/signup`). This route will process user data: `fullName`, `email`, `phone`, `password`, and `currency`, encrypting the password with bcrypt and generating a token.\n\n\n**Generating Account Numbers**\n\nI\'m now focusing on account number generation for new users, ensuring uniqueness through a check. The system generates an unverified user and returns details, including a message to select an OTP delivery method. For `POST /api/auth/send-otp`, I will validate the user, generate and hash a 6-digit OTP, storing it with an expiry. It will also log the OTP to the console, and then it calls the email or SMS/WhatsApp APIs. The mock email is also working nicely, now.\n\n\n**Implementing OTP Verification**\n\nI\'ve completed the OTP sending logic. It checks for Twilio config; if present, it sends via Twilio; otherwise, it logs a mock message. I now need to implement the verification route, `POST /api/auth/verify-otp`. It will fetch the user, validate the OTP against the hashed version using bcrypt, mark the user as verified, clear OTP data, and generate a session token or secure token/signed cookie for authorization, which feels robust!\n\n\n**Defining Login Flow**\n\nI\'m now concentrating on the login process. The `POST /api/auth/login` route will handle standard credentials validation. If successful, I\'ll return user details (ID, email, phone, and verification status) to prompt a frontend redirect to `/verify-method`. This approach ensures all logins go through OTP verification, keeping the authorization flow consistent.\n\n\n**Defining Transaction APIs**\n\nI am now outlining the API endpoints for transaction management. I\'m starting with `POST /api/transactions/fund`, which handles updating user balances and saving credit transactions. The `POST /api/transactions/transfer` endpoint requires balance validation, reference generation, and debit transaction saving. I\'ve also planned a `GET /api/transactions` endpoint for fetching transactions, using query params for filtering and pagination. This should provide a comprehensive transaction history. I\'m now implementing the APIs, beginning with the fund and transfer, respectively.\n\n\n**Defining OTP Formats**\n\nI\'ve specified the account number format: `ACB-2026-` followed by five uppercase alphanumeric characters or numbers, such as `ACB-2026-H8YTR`. This meets the required pattern. I\'ve also defined OTP message templates for both email and WhatsApp/SMS, ensuring they are clear, specific, and secure. Now, I\'m focusing on the Nodemailer and Twilio configurations to implement these templates perfectly. I\'m taking the time to precisely define the configuration for both services.\n\n\n**Configuring Email & SMS**\n\nI\'ve just solidified the email and SMS configurations. I\'ve implemented lazy initialization for both Nodemailer and Twilio clients. This prevents startup crashes if credentials aren\'t set, while still allowing seamless integration when they are. I\'ve defined functions for getting the transporter and the Twilio client, ensuring smooth conditional initialization. Now, I\'m verifying and fine-tuning these configurations.\n\n\n"
    modified_response: "I will create the backend server `/server.ts`. This file handles the entire REST API suite for authentication, OTP generation & dispatch, user queries, profile changes, wallet fund ingestion, and recipient funds transfers, while seamlessly integrating the Vite developer middleware for development and static compilation paths for build assets in production."
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 10
      nanos: 294928515
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
