    thinking: "**Refining The Login Card**\n\nOkay, so I\'ve updated the login card\'s styling. I\'ve switched from the more generic `rounded-2xl shadow-xl` to the more tailored `rounded-[32px] border border-blue-100 shadow-2xl shadow-blue-200/50`. The goal is a noticeably sleeker, more polished presentation. I\'m keen to see how this translates into the overall look and feel. Let\'s dig deeper!\n\n\n**Analyzing Dashboard Layout**\n\nI\'m now focusing on adapting the dashboard view. My plan involves modifying `src/components/DashboardView.tsx` to accommodate elegant side navigation on desktop and bottom navigation on mobile. I am carefully analyzing the \"Sleek Interface\" dashboard\'s column layout, specifically the `grid grid-cols-12 gap-8 flex-1` structure, which includes a `col-span-8` for the left panel and a `col-span-4` for the right. I\'m taking note of our current tabs.\n\n\n**Designing Application Layout**\n\nI\'m deep in the layout design. My thought process is now on the `TransferView` component and implementing our layout strategy. The plan is to create a dynamic layout that adapts to the user\'s screen size. On desktops (md+), I\'m aiming for a sidebar navigation with an active indicator, and a two-column view for the home tab, with a balance card, recent transactions, and custom actions. Mobile views will have bottom navigation with a single-column layout. I\'m excited about the adaptive elegance!\n\n\n**Implementing Navigation System**\n\nI\'ve been working on the sidebar navigation, aiming for a design that is both functional and aesthetically pleasing. I\'m focusing on adapting the \"Sleek Interface\" sidebar\'s structure: a branded header, followed by navigation links. I intend to use buttons linked to state selectors, specifically `setActiveTab(\"home\" | \"send\" | \"history\" | \"profile\")`, to manage tab selections. I\'m excited about the perfect adaptation. The intention is 100%, and respects the existing routing.\n\n\n**Updating Security Implementation**\n\nI\'m now focused on the security status display within the dashboard. Instead of the hardcoded \"Verified via SMS\", I\'ll dynamically render the active verification method from the database. I\'m keen to implement this dynamic display in `src/components/DashboardView.tsx`, after taking a look at tab icons. I\'m excited to ensure it\'s both functional and perfectly sleek, which enhances the overall user experience.\n\n\n**Revising Dashboard Structure**\n\nI\'m now revising the structural components of `DashboardView`. I\'m going for a cleaner, more tailored look and feel. The intention is to create a background color `#E8F4FD`, and to ensure the components are nicely positioned. The plan includes the creation of a sidebar, header, and main panel within a flexible, full-height container. The mobile view should have a bottom navigation bar.\n\n\n**Evaluating Layout Integration**\n\nOkay, I\'m now integrating the layout components. I\'ve been reviewing the code snippet related to navigation and recent transactions to ensure a clean implementation. I\'m focusing on the \"Recent Transactions\" section and confirming its presentation in the theme. My plan is to split the `home` view into a majestic layout, with a grid on larger screens.\n\n\n**Preparing Dashboard Implementation**\n\nI\'m now carefully preparing for the `DashboardView.tsx` replacement, to ensure mobile single-column stacking. I\'m focusing on exact import statements, checking file structure and paths, particularly those in `src/components/DashboardView.tsx` lines 1-12, before rewriting the file. I\'m taking extreme care, so that the new implementation matches the theme perfectly.\n\n\n"
