    summary: "The error message \"Cannot set property fetch\" or \"Cannot set property of undefined\" typically occurs in JavaScript when you try to assign a value to a property of a variable that is currently `undefined` or `null`.[1][2][3] This means the object or variable you\'re trying to modify doesn\'t exist or hasn\'t been properly initialized.\n\nWhen this error appears in conjunction with libraries like `node-fetch`, `twilio`, `mongoose`, or `google/genai`, several specific scenarios are common:\n\n### Common Causes Across Libraries\n\n*   **Uninitialized Variables/Objects:** The most frequent cause is attempting to access or set a property on an object that hasn\'t been created, loaded, or assigned yet. For example, if a variable meant to hold an instance of a library client is `undefined`, any attempt to call a method or set a property on it will fail.[1]\n*   **Incorrect Scope or Timing:** In browser environments, running JavaScript before the Document Object Model (DOM) is fully loaded can cause errors when trying to manipulate non-existent elements.[2][3] While less common for the listed Node.js-focused libraries, similar timing issues can occur if dependencies aren\'t loaded before being used.\n*   **Typos or Misconfigurations:** Simple spelling errors in property names or misconfigurations in how a library is initialized or used can lead to attempts to access non-existent properties.[2]\n\n### Specific to `node-fetch`\n\n*   **`fetch` Not Defined in Node.js (Older Versions):** In Node.js environments prior to version 18, the native `fetch` API was not globally available.[4][5] Developers used `node-fetch` as a polyfill. If `node-fetch` is not correctly imported (e.g., `const fetch = require(\'node-fetch\');`) or installed, `fetch` will be `undefined`, leading to errors if code tries to use it.[4]\n*   **Overwriting Global `fetch`:** If you encounter \"Cannot set property fetch of #<Window> which has only a getter\"[6], it indicates an attempt to redefine or set the `fetch` property on the global `Window` object in a browser-like environment, where `fetch` is often a read-only global.\n*   **Module System Conflicts:** Issues with `import` vs. `require` or attempting to use `node-fetch` in client-side code (where native `fetch` already exists) can cause unexpected behavior.[4][7]\n*   **Internal `node-fetch` Issues:** Some specific errors like \"Cannot read property \'Symbol(requestOptions)\' of undefined\" or \"Cannot read properties of undefined (reading \'Symbol(Body internals)\')\" have been reported in `node-fetch` in certain Node.js versions or when other libraries modify `Object.prototype`.[8][9]\n\n### Specific to `twilio`\n\n*   **Uninitialized `Twilio.Device`:** An error like \"Cannot set property \'audio\' of undefined\" when instantiating `twilio-client` suggests that the `Device` object might not be correctly initialized or that a required configuration property (`audio`) is being accessed before it\'s set.[10]\n*   **API Client Initialization:** If you\'re using `fetch` directly with Twilio APIs (instead of their official SDK), \"access denied\" or similar network errors can occur if API base URLs (especially for regional endpoints) or authentication tokens are incorrect or missing.[11][12]\n\n### Specific to `mongoose`\n\n*   **Schema Strictness:** Mongoose schemas are strict by default, meaning you cannot set properties on a Mongoose document that are not defined in its schema.[13][14] To add un-schemed properties, you might need to convert the Mongoose document to a plain JavaScript object (e.g., `doc.toObject()` or `JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(doc))`) or explicitly set the `strict` option to `false` in your schema.[13][14]\n*   **Saving on Schema vs. Document:** Trying to save a subdocument or property directly on the `Schema` object itself rather than on a newly created or retrieved `Document` instance will result in this error.[15]\n*   **Undefined Query Parameters:** Errors like \"Cannot set property \'_id\' of undefined\" can happen when querying with `_id` and the provided value is `undefined`.[16]\n\n### Specific to `google/genai`\n\n*   **`fetch` Not Defined (Node.js < 18):** Similar to `node-fetch`, the `@google/generative-ai` library relies on the global `fetch` API. If you are running Node.js versions older than 18, you will encounter a `ReferenceError: fetch is not defined`.[5][17] Upgrading Node.js to version 18 or higher typically resolves this.\n*   **Network/Proxy Issues:** Errors like \"Error fetching from [API endpoint]: fetch failed\"[18] often point to underlying network problems, firewall restrictions, or incorrect proxy configurations preventing the API calls from reaching Google\'s servers.[19]\n*   **Unsupported User Location:** The Gemini API might have regional restrictions, leading to \"User location is not supported for the API use\" errors if you try to access it from a restricted region.[20]\n*   **Incorrect API Key or Model ID:** Ensuring your API key is correct, active, and the client library is properly initialized with the right model identifier is crucial.[18]\n\n### Troubleshooting Steps\n\n1.  **Check for `undefined` or `null`:** Before attempting to set a property, ensure the object or variable you\'re targeting is not `undefined` or `null`. Use `console.log()` to inspect its value.\n2.  **Verify Imports/Requires:** Double-check that all necessary libraries (`node-fetch`, `twilio`, `mongoose`, `google/genai`) are correctly imported or required at the top of your file and installed in your project.\n3.  **Node.js Version:** If using `fetch` or a library that depends on it in Node.js, ensure your Node.js version is 18 or higher.[5][17]\n4.  **Library Initialization:** Confirm that you are correctly initializing client instances for Twilio, Mongoose models, or Google Generative AI.\n5.  **Mongoose Schema:** If using Mongoose, ensure that the properties you are trying to set are defined in your schema, or handle them as plain JavaScript objects if schema strictness is an issue.[13][14]\n6.  **Network and Proxies:** For `fetch`-related errors, investigate network connectivity, firewall rules, and proxy settings that might be blocking outbound API requests.\n7.  **API Keys and Endpoints:** Verify that API keys are correct and active, and that you are using the appropriate API endpoints, especially for regional services.[12][18]\n\nSources:\n[1] [medium.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQEQJDLvsFBQNBqKNAGp6wOkj0us_GRYn2rt7SYwyENn-OcLgxeTxiyId-rMBWN0875mAUIicZSfvxJRiAdvZtOjbgE4b8RkC7GYxW0Dqo2hXrTcAkDyvbUB1FsDMA4Pswg8N2md4POJttFfITVRsls7QRCjrSmPIzoolrkj8GNau6xgdat9ljp_8obt7E2GHG4vXqmGKKMBEs-qlCBVlwTSFfkPejQJQU8JQCU7XZA=)\n[2] [kinsta.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQH4C0ZNUPxhbM1BTIxbJCHDOSRwU8PWtwcfnkWlTwdnp9CTTYCXTVtcQ-Ezi91z7QSRO8BXF3ntg2g14WW7DHcJwTRdUkzsrAm29kdjyhZwAbgU9fzyLLuIeVYPy6Ceb3RgGJ8dxM09EHP9tcxbUkQQW0dK9HwRJI8=)\n[3] [quora.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQHToD_0F1Vy1xuVRQduYNDGK9-h10merl46NsJQcr4yeOPe9Yqw9G5KmQrqlaMu2qPTc_df-Stm0m6BVgn22CEq33VbSExwV0ErtLkTzeSJx8Q6w0bbSdy75D2_LSkPRk1b9hgSUm913z05mgX8h8ZViov1BKWRmQaZwxoS_3GL2gL1KAplPinzQfEsJgWuQZx1xC7IbSbA)\n[4] [manektech.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQFed8C1WhwHOzTylzJ4eOh5W8szXoDfe4IyW5v8oSjPRTdvpru58Sp9v7sjbIjFfOYl4AYq0UQcb2hR7_Z5bN4ItGhyt5GQ7XLO9udmV4WQ83Z_-T6axTLcWVpX-AL4iSt1HuxJhUSblw9A-FlMQYlYxn63iXp_E0wwuCSKy3tJpc2sfETHVZvQjRuaQcXc9myOatc81Rk=)\n[5] [stackoverflow.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQHFVYWYZSWSTZwtr-8DqNZW617JIQpmz2imO5QxtqQOkNoKU93Weuthlw1r4ImuUy86sRGh8jUNv-AzM-EiCwzZXcAq96zFgvDuxosqLmSwn1Xn_BI9a0-LSKJzlueJ8JpBB9P-OYtvGtscwEAhs9oryb9d8huW2j_boIoohh7P72GYTS5632rAXJOE7LllPL1jj_B8UA-96BKWNaLkPd1Qzak87IDTm98bWHt4cVw2krUFLR4=)\n[6] [google.dev](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AU