    thinking: "**Investigating `fetch` Conflicts**\n\nI\'m digging into potential conflicts related to the `fetch` function, prompted by an observation in the `node-fetch` README. The concern is with assigning `globalThis.fetch`. Currently, I\'m trying to identify other packages that might be similarly assigning to `globalThis.fetch`.\n\n\n**Unraveling Node-Fetch Browser Presence**\n\nI\'m puzzled. My focus is now on how `node-fetch` could be involved in a browser context. I\'ve been tracing the imports to see if a package that uses `node-fetch` sneaks into the frontend through a dependency. I haven\'t seen any evidence of server-side packages like `@google/genai` or `mongoose` being imported. I\'ve re-checked that `src/db.ts` is only imported by `server.ts`, confirming no client-side usage.\n\n\n**Tracing Potential Fetch Bundling**\n\nOkay, I\'ve shifted gears again. I\'m now checking the `.vite` cache and Vite output for any signs of `node-fetch` or similar packages being bundled unexpectedly. I\'ve re-examined the imports of `src/db.ts`, and checked the imports of `src/db.ts` to see if it imports node packages, which it does: `mongoose`, `fs`, `path`, and `bcryptjs`. Also, I\'ve confirmed that no React files import `src/db.ts` or its dependencies. I\'m exploring if a Node package is being imported under the `.env` directory. And I am going to search search results to find a related error. Now, I am going to search for the error message itself.\n\n\n**Pinpointing the `fetch` Origin**\n\nI\'ve hit a potential breakthrough. My focus has narrowed to pinpointing where `node-fetch` might be loaded in the browser. I\'m exploring the possibility of an older version of `node-fetch` with a `globalThis.fetch =` assignment being bundled somehow, possibly through a dependency like `twilio`. I\'m going to search for the error, as well as checking the `package-lock.json` file for further clues. I\'m also confirming I have `\"twilio\": \"^6.0.2\"` in `package.json`.\n\n\n"
