  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Investigating a Bug**\n\nI\'m puzzled. Exit code 1 indicates no matches, but why is `formdata-polyfill` erroring if it\'s not bundled? I need to understand this. My focus is on the unexpected error with the polyfill. Is the server code somehow running client-side? No, it\'s not. That eliminates one possible issue. I need to dig deeper.\n\n\n**Tracing the Server Startup**\n\nI\'m now tracing the server startup sequence to pinpoint where the `formdata-polyfill` might be getting invoked. I\'ve confirmed that `server.ts` is running under `node` and using `tsx`. I\'ve examined `server.ts`\'s imports and confirmed that `@google/genai` is *not* directly imported there. My next step is to see if it\'s imported dynamically or by another file. I\'m focusing on the server\'s initial execution path.\n\n\n**Clarifying the Execution Environment**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the execution environment to see where this `Window` object is originating. The key question now is whether the error is triggered in the browser or Node.js. If it\'s Node.js, the presence of a `Window` object would be highly unusual. I\'m exploring the possibility of some code redefining `window` or `global.window` to point to the server global, which would lead to this unexpected behavior.\n\n\n**Confirming the Fetch Hypothesis**\n\nI\'ve got it! I\'ve confirmed that Node.js 18+ has a built-in, read-only `fetch` on `globalThis`. If any library tries to redefine `globalThis.fetch` or `global.fetch`, Node will throw a TypeError. This perfectly explains the error with `formdata-polyfill`! I\'m verifying this hypothesis, focusing on how `FormData.js` attempts to assign to `global.fetch`. The `#<Window>` part is still a puzzle.\n\n\n**Analyzing the Bundling Process**\n\nI\'m now investigating how `FormData.js` is ending up in the browser environment. The `#<Window>` part definitely indicates a browser context, which is the puzzling part. I\'m focusing on the bundling process within Vite. Perhaps there\'s an indirect import or a polyfill that\'s introducing `FormData.js`. I\'m starting to explore how the server-side libraries relate to the client-side bundling, and looking at the import graph.\n\n\n**Tracing the Import Chain**\n\nI\'m now tracing the import chain to understand how `db.ts` might be involved. The recent checks revealed no direct imports of `db.ts` within the `src/` directory. I\'m focusing on how Vite handles `db.ts`, considering automatic inclusions or alternative entry points to the application. I\'m looking at `index.html` to confirm whether it may point to `db.ts`. I need to ensure it\'s not being processed unexpectedly during the bundling.\n\n\n**Exploring the Frontend**\n\nI\'m now investigating potential front-end interference. My analysis of `main.tsx`\'s imports confirms that `db.ts` isn\'t directly imported into `index.html`, and a dynamic `import(\"./db\")` isn\'t present in `src/`. I\'m now focusing on the possibility of external factors, like the Google AI Studio build environment itself, patching the `window` object and impacting the `fetch` assignment in `FormData.js`. I\'m wondering if a library is failing because of the AI Studio preview.\n\n\n**Identifying the Origin**\n\nI\'ve realized the Studio\'s iframe likely has a read-only `fetch` getter, which leads to the error when a script tries to assign to it. I\'m focusing on pinpointing the script doing this assignment, and I\'m going to start by searching for any direct assignments of `window.fetch =` or `.fetch =` in our code base. I also need to identify which packages we\'re loading in the browser to narrow my search. I\'ve already confirmed `react`, `react-dom`, `motion`, and `lucide-react` do *not* have any `fetch` assignments. I\'m now exploring the possibility of overriding or redefining `globalThis.fetch` to circumvent the error. Is it configurable? I\'m asking whether this iframe read-only getter is actually overrideable.\n\n\n**Implementing a Workaround**\n\nI\'m now implementing a `window.fetch` setter at the top of `src/main.tsx` to redefine the property as configurable. My focus is on intercepting the getter-only `window.fetch` to allow assignment. The code is:\n\n```javascript\nlet _nativeFetch = window.fetch;\nObject.defineProperty(window, \'fetch\', {\n  get() {\n    return _nativeFetch;\n  },\n  set(newFetch) {\n    _nativeFetch = newFetch;\n  },\n  configurable: true,\n  enumerable: true\n});\n```\n\nThis should allow libraries to assign to `window.fetch` without error, resolving the `formdata-polyfill` issue. It\'s an elegant fix!\n\n\n**Verifying the Iframe Context**\n\nI\'m now checking if the iframe\'s `window.fetch` is non-configurable, which would block my proposed workaround. If `configurable` is `false`, `Object.defineProperty` will fail. I\'m focusing on confirming if the Studio iframe has enforced this restriction. I need to know how to prevent or override it if it is non-configurable. I\'m confirming this by verifying whether a direct assignment to `window.fetch` will work, and checking with a quick browser console test.\n\n\n**Analyzing the Scope**\n\nI\'m now investigating the scope of the error to understand where `FormData.js` is being loaded. I\'m focusing on why a server-side file, like `db.ts`, might be bundled for the client-side. The key question is whether a server-side file could be bundled at all. If it can, why are we seeing that error? Now I\'m checking the Vite configuration for unexpected includes or exclusions.\n\n\n**Examining the Import Path**\n\nI\'m now investigating the potential bundling of `server.ts` or its imports for the client-side. I\'ve re-examined the execution path and confirmed that `tsx server.ts` runs directly in Node.js. My focus is on understanding the import path. The error is occurring in the browser, which is where the browser is picking up the `FormData.js` file. The server shouldn\'t cause this error. I am now checking the browser console\'s import.\n\n\n"
