    summary: "When encountering the error \"Cannot set property fetch of\", it typically indicates an attempt to reassign or modify the `fetch` function in an environment where it is a read-only property. This often occurs in web browsers when interacting with `window.fetch`, but similar issues can arise in Node.js environments with `node-fetch` or the global `fetch` object.\n\nHere\'s a breakdown of the issue and common contexts:\n\n### In Browser Environments (`window.fetch`)\n\nThe most frequent occurrence of this error is \"Uncaught TypeError: Cannot set property fetch of #<Window> which has only a getter\"[1][2][3][4][5][6]. This means that the `fetch` property on the `window` object is implemented with a getter but no setter, making it non-writable by direct assignment.\n\n**Common Scenarios:**\n\n*   **Browser Extensions and Userscripts:** Developers trying to intercept or polyfill `window.fetch` within content scripts or userscripts (like those managed by Tampermonkey or Greasemonkey) often encounter this restriction. Browsers protect built-in global properties like `fetch` from being easily overridden for security and stability reasons[5].\n*   **Third-Party Libraries:** Some JavaScript libraries might try to \"proxy\" or wrap `window.fetch` during their initialization. If `window.fetch` is protected, this attempt will fail[3][7]. For instance, `vConsole` has addressed this issue by checking if the property is writable before attempting to proxy it[3][7].\n*   **Google AI Studio:** Users working with Google AI Studio have reported this specific `TypeError` in recent times, sometimes alongside issues related to API quotas or general platform instability[1][2][8][9][4][10].\n\n**Potential Solutions/Workarounds for Browsers:**\n\n*   **Using `Object.defineProperty()` cautiously:** While direct assignment fails, one might attempt to redefine `window.fetch` using `Object.defineProperty()`. However, this will only work if the property is configurable. If it\'s not, even `defineProperty` will throw an error. A robust solution, if proxying is necessary, involves checking the property descriptor first[3].\n*   **Executing code in the page\'s context:** For userscripts, executing the modification logic using `window.eval()` can sometimes bypass restrictions by running the code directly within the webpage\'s scope, rather than the isolated content script scope[11].\n*   **Updating or configuring libraries:** If a third-party library is causing the error, checking for updates to the library or configuration options that disable its `fetch` overriding behavior may resolve the issue[3][7].\n\n### In Node.js Environments (`global` or `node-fetch`)\n\nIn Node.js, `fetch` might be available natively (in newer versions) or through a polyfill like `node-fetch`. The error \"Cannot set property \'fetch\' of undefined\" can occur if you try to assign to `fetch` directly without properly referencing the global object or a module\'s export.\n\n**Common Scenarios:**\n\n*   **Incorrect Global Assignment:** If you intend to set a custom `fetch` implementation globally, you should assign it to `global.fetch` or `globalThis.fetch` (which is the ES2020 standard way to access the global object in any environment)[12]. Directly assigning to a variable named `fetch` that hasn\'t been declared or is not part of the global scope will lead to an undefined reference error[12].\n*   **`node-fetch` integration:** When using `node-fetch`, you typically import it and use the imported function, rather than trying to globally set `fetch` unless you are explicitly polyfilling the global scope.\n\n**Potential Solutions/Workarounds for Node.js:**\n\n*   **Use `globalThis.fetch = customFetch;`**: To correctly set a global `fetch` implementation in Node.js (and browsers that support `globalThis`), use `globalThis`[12].\n*   **Ensure `node-fetch` is imported and used correctly:** If you\'re using `node-fetch`, import it and call it directly (e.g., `import fetch from \'node-fetch\';`).\n\nIn summary, the \"Cannot set property fetch of\" error primarily stems from attempts to modify a read-only `fetch` property, especially on the browser\'s `window` object. Understanding the environment (browser vs. Node.js) and the nature of `fetch`\'s implementation (getter-only vs. assignable) is key to resolving such issues.\n\nSources:\n[1] [google.dev](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQHhuJLOFR-TG0O_eqCzPTqC4u6nh_Cy3EMoHSyAoU5roNwCrK8jv9xGRrN8g0ogbfau2hCkFjMse1EDYpor7Lq1Nf9dd6w0Er2DfDeTH7f7HI6un9gJooHgFWwEgnOU5Xy7mzRyBI_ayPEHP10atXWIRw_VBEVfdC0DKoQUnmjpNt1PfFKgnkmfVVTuEXvDEnEeJqrnsVAo-76ZQAg_r2pTQuptWBagj2P-jZzkWMk16Q==)\n[2] [stackoverflow.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQGBebEAeAw0TnBz8Uqlu4_jkVSoMqhWJ4XCU4znaoGG2NvHZvRoaEo0dmHNAu4ePgs9oXmtmBqHx1Ost9N1g7Ec8_RQlSkWS_O1O5KVe3uN7sSr2k3exV7p1y-fQoM5gFk_Zql_nqD1rt9py7Y7ig9pXqbswFDz2JKLZPDlARnieFHzoQ==)\n[3] [github.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQEGpsBzR2u88ZHfEkZlOJNFTFqTH4bme1e0SrGo95sv_EVVN6m7RNTBA8SOjCsZkb40OJ3hUABc2QQe2FtHY2w3Oqzp18_uCJ9CclTyDjP-RWO-60YqFOHPv-5p4MXBT7Ghvj802BYU4sM=)\n[4] [google.dev](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQF_oNpEKEYfPbyySNLhNq_JvcI1BPaogRJYlg7MfSlwUXFqLZ8iuTBVSdE-596Ty7Ojb9BEpKswcfyMBDmXxVacUumnNaAc-eJvNT95vND6klOUuglOwzw1wkj_poz_IWFmL7FRPJYBgHEl-qTlrMzv4t7B9WefLgDt9JFeXo02C0hjLTucMuX14pGYNeTf1mX4_Zuvn4m4XUxdl6bNwvWhqGwvKvlkAGDadGiyX-MibY7fNuFZ7wVu13oYbZmHddpnWEmqYDdM)\n[5] [github.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQGA2NHeL0Dko0OsRJU7s5qivWeC65_87iAjS5wxHFPfZc1SERhTSrKIN9_LGNP7rBNHF_sh7Z3QD_gUGpRAOSSFcCZe_kpdiRSjgD7udQefCDykOrNP8wrIwz0_WTptMhD_WYpcH5snFU20RA==)\n[6] [github.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQFeexN1L-uAIo4fPUL-hXhs9Jv-RpK7TFG0jc5LNMuetrEInLSg8RnFVmJd5LE1XY2WrWU8Pb9QKttHmlYfYD9K3X1tD7z2rOQx0ojyGsKNTphAIZrQZboJfywKfn-9P