    thinking: "**Analyzing the Script**\n\nI\'ve been examining the JavaScript within `index.html`. My current focus is on the interception of `window.fetch`. It appears a proxy is being set up, potentially to monitor or modify network requests. I\'m trying to decipher the exact purpose and how it\'s implemented. Specifically, the use of `Object.defineProperty` intrigues me, I\'m trying to understand why this method was chosen.\n\n\n**Investigating the Error**\n\nI\'ve hit a potential snag. I\'ve realized the script might fail if `window.fetch` is already non-configurable. This could happen in certain iframe sandbox environments or if platform scripts have preconfigured `fetch`. My thought is, that `Object.defineProperty` will throw a `TypeError` in such scenarios, which is not handled by the `try...catch` block. I need to figure out how to work around this.\n\n\n**Addressing the Uncaught Error**\n\nI realized my previous assessment was incomplete. While the `try...catch` handles the initial `TypeError`, the underlying issue remains: `window.fetch` is still non-writable after the failed redefinition. This means subsequent attempts to overwrite `fetch`, such as by libraries like `formdata-polyfill`, will trigger an \"Uncaught TypeError\". I\'m now trying to understand why this polyfill is being invoked in the client. I haven\'t found a direct import of `formdata-polyfill` in the client-side code, and I need to determine where it\'s being loaded from. I\'m checking other potential locations such as the `/dist` and `vite` directories.\n\n\n**Tracing the Dependency**\n\nI\'m now tracing the dependency chain to pinpoint the origin of the problematic `formdata-polyfill` call, and how it is being invoked. My recent focus is on checking which packages in `/src/components/` and `/` may be causing this. I\'ve narrowed it down and it\'s looking like it\'s a specific import from a particular module. I\'m doing a deep dive into the code for it to identify the exact cause.\n\n\n**Pinpointing the Import**\n\nI\'m now investigating imports across `src/context/`, `/src/App.tsx`, and `/src/main.tsx`. I\'m using `grep` to identify external imports, focusing on dependencies beyond `./` and `react`. The goal is to determine if a related import is involved. I\'ve confirmed that `src/db.ts` is *not* imported by any client file, and Vite does not bundle `server.ts`. I am currently searching the network tab and `index.html` for `formdata-polyfill` loads and imports.\n\n\n**Identifying the Proxy**\n\nI\'m now exploring the potential of intercepting `Object.defineProperty` or creating a robust proxy, or a shadow getter/setter, before any script loading occurs. The goal is to make both `globalThis.fetch` and `window.fetch` writable. I believe this might circumvent the underlying issue of the non-configurable or read-only `fetch` implementation. I\'m investigating how Vite handles global imports and if it could be related to the `formdata-polyfill` loading.\n\n\n**Refining the Approach**\n\nI\'m now refining the approach. The key is defining a *setter* for `fetch` on `window` and `globalThis` *before* other scripts are executed. If `window.fetch` is read-only, this allows any assignment to use our setter instead of throwing an error. This seems like a potent solution. I\'m exploring the implications of redefining the property, specifically concerning cases where the original descriptor has `configurable: false`. I\'m verifying what will happen if I call `Object.defineProperty(window, \'fetch\', ...)` and the existing property is non-configurable.\n\n\n**Exploring Configuration**\n\nI\'m now investigating the configurability of `window.fetch` within the iframe environment of Google AI Studio. My key question: is it possible to redefine `fetch` directly on `window`, or are we restricted to `globalThis`, `global`, or `Window.prototype`? My plan is to determine the precise location of `fetch` within the prototype chain, and its configuration. I\'m focusing on the implications of sandboxed iframes. I\'m considering the potential impact of security wrappers on standard browser behavior.\n\n\n**Confirming the Scope**\n\nI\'m now confirming the scope of my changes. I\'m focusing on ensuring the redefined `fetch` getter and setter are consistently applied across both `window` and `globalThis`. My thought is to establish a robust and universal method for intercepting all requests, irrespective of how `fetch` is accessed. I\'m carefully reviewing the try/catch logic to ensure correct behavior.\n\n\n**Considering Alternatives**\n\nI\'m now contemplating alternative strategies. The previous method won\'t work if `window.fetch` is non-configurable, as setting the descriptor will throw an error. I am currently considering if defining `globalThis.fetch` or `global.fetch` could work instead, or if there\'s any other way to bypass the limitation. I am looking for ways to redirect library lookups away from `window` directly.\n\n\n**Defining the Proxy**\n\nMy focus has shifted to the `formdata-polyfill`\'s use of `globalThis`, `self`, and `global`. The polyfill checks these to determine the global object. I\'m now exploring how to make `globalThis` or `global` point to a proxy. Since `globalThis` itself is read-only, I\'m considering redefining `global` on `window` to control its behavior. Specifically, I\'m working through how the polyfill will interact with our injected proxy instead of the default `window.fetch`.\n\n\n"
