    thinking: "**Analyzing the Script**\n\nI\'ve been examining the provided JavaScript snippet, focusing on the `Object.defineProperty` usage. I\'m trying to understand the implications of redefining `window.fetch`. My focus is on the `writable`, `configurable`, and `enumerable` flags. I believe these flags will show how the modification affects the behavior of the `fetch` function.\n\n\n**Investigating the Exception**\n\nI\'ve hit a crucial point. The `Object.defineProperty` call will throw an exception if `window.fetch` has only a getter and is non-configurable, as expected. This caught exception makes `window.fetch` read-only. I\'m exploring intercepting or preventing the error globally. While modern JS prevents overriding `typeof globalThis`, I\'m pondering whether a setter for `fetch` could be defined on `window`\'s prototype, despite the direct `fetch` property being non-configurable, with only a getter.\n\n\n**Exploring Prototype Solutions**\n\nI\'m now focusing on where `window.fetch` is defined. The fact it\'s likely on `Window.prototype` is key. I\'m wondering if a setter on the prototype could work to intercept attempted modifications. However, if `fetch` is non-configurable, even a prototype setter is blocked. It seems any direct assignment like `global.fetch = function...` will still fail. The `configurable: false` restriction is the major hurdle.\n\n\n**Unraveling Package Dependencies**\n\nI\'m now tracing dependencies, suspecting a package is the culprit. I\'m investigating why `formdata-polyfill` is client-side. My recent investigation revealed that `@google/genai` indirectly uses `node-fetch`. Also, I am checking if Vite is pre-bundling any packages. I am looking for something imported in a `node_modules` file which is bundled by Vite for the client. The investigation narrows down to tracing how these packages are incorporated, and whether they involve client-side pre-bundling.\n\n\n**Uncovering Pre-Optimization**\n\nNow I\'m diving into how Vite handles dependencies. It seems Vite\'s pre-optimization process is the key. My understanding is that Vite scans `package.json` and the codebase for dependencies, pre-optimizing them in anticipation of use. This means even if a package isn\'t directly imported in the main entry, Vite might still process it. I\'m exploring whether this pre-optimization can lead to client-side issues, even if the packages are meant for the server. I am trying to determine if a dependency of a Vite plugin could somehow load the problematic packages, even if not imported.\n\n\n**Pinpointing the Source**\n\nI\'m now investigating the origin of the \"Cannot set property fetch\" error. I\'m exploring if it\'s originating from `formdata-polyfill`, the AI Studio iframe, a Chrome extension, or a standard library. I\'m considering scenarios in Chrome/Electron, and searching for the error online to understand standard behaviors. My focus is on scripts loaded into the iframe by the platform, and if they may be interfering with `fetch` reassignment. I\'m trying to determine if the platform might have defined `window.fetch` as read-only, causing the error.\n\n\n"
