tion failures gracefully, falling back to local JSON storage if needed.\n\n\n**Refactoring the CRUD functions**\n\nI\'m now carefully reviewing the CRUD functions, starting with `findUserByEmail`. I\'m going to refactor it to use the new `shouldAttemptMongo()` helper, streamlining the logic and improving error handling. I\'m focusing on ensuring graceful fallbacks to the local JSON store whenever MongoDB connectivity issues arise. I will examine how this structure will now be propagated to the other CRUD calls.\n\n\n\n**Streamlining the Database Calls**\n\nI\'m now implementing the `findUserById` function, I\'m adapting the same error handling and fallback strategy. I\'m taking the opportunity to use the `shouldAttemptMongo()` helper function to simplify the code, and ensure a smooth fall-back process. I\'m focusing on ensuring graceful fallbacks to the local JSON store whenever MongoDB connectivity issues arise. I will now examine this structure will now be propagated to the other CRUD calls.\n\n\n**Implementing the `createUser` function**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the `createUser` function. I\'m building it to reflect the pattern from `findUserById`, including the `shouldAttemptMongo()` helper. This guarantees that MongoDB connection attempts are conditional. I\'ll include the error handling, which falls back to the JSON database. I am noting how this will now be integrated with the other database CRUD calls.\n\n\n**Constructing the createUser Implementation**\n\nI\'m now integrating the `createUser` function. I am now incorporating the user data, mapping the values into the schema, and writing them to either the MongoDB or JSON store. I\'m taking care to format the response to match existing data structures. I\'m focusing on ensuring data integrity is maintained across both data stores.\n\n\n\n**Implementing the updateUser function**\n\nI\'m now incorporating the `updateUser` function. My focus is on ensuring data persistence in both MongoDB and JSON stores, mirroring the pattern in `createUser` with conditional MongoDB attempts via `shouldAttemptMongo()`. I\'m adapting the `try/catch` block for MongoDB operations, ensuring graceful fallback to local JSON storage upon connection errors. I am focusing on ensuring that the `_id` is retained.\n\n\n**Adapting the updateUser call**\n\nI\'m now completing the `updateUser` function, ensuring both MongoDB and JSON data stores are consistently updated. I\'ve integrated a conditional MongoDB update based on `shouldAttemptMongo()`. I\'m using a `try/catch` block for MongoDB operations, ensuring seamless fallback to local JSON storage on connection issues. I\'m focusing on preserving the original `_id` value and handling the `otpExpiry` date conversion correctly.\n\n\n**Finalizing the database calls**\n\nI\'m now implementing the `addTransaction` function, focusing on the same robust `try/catch` and `shouldAttemptMongo()` strategy used in previous calls. I\'m adapting the logic to properly handle MongoDB transactions, and ensuring seamless fallback to local JSON storage on connection issues. I\'m focusing on preserving data integrity.\n\nI am now completing the implementation of `addTransaction`, including integrating the data mapping, and implementing the error handling to gracefully fall back to local JSON storage. I\'ve ensured a consistent structure and now need to implement `getTransactions()`.\n\n\n**Implementing the `getTransactions` function**\n\nI\'m now completing `getTransactions()`, ensuring data integrity across both MongoDB and JSON storage. I\'m carefully implementing the MongoDB queries with `try/catch` blocks and the `shouldAttemptMongo()` helper. This guarantees graceful fallback to JSON if MongoDB fails. I\'m focusing on adapting the response format for consistent data representation.\n\nI am now implementing the final error handling and fallback logic. I\'m focusing on formatting the data so it can be integrated with the front-end user interface.\n\n\n**Refining the transaction calls**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the transaction handling, specifically the `addTransaction` and `getTransactions` calls, ensuring consistent data handling. I\'m focusing on the data type conversions and integrations for the `addTransaction` function to persist both MongoDB and local JSON data. I\'m also examining the `getTransactions` function to ensure accurate results, and consistent pagination and search for the user interface.\n\n\n**Finalizing the Transaction Data**\n\nI\'m now implementing the MongoDB calls for transactions, including data handling for `addTransaction` and `getTransactions`. I\'m focused on correctly formatting the transactions and data type conversions, ensuring consistency across both MongoDB and JSON storage. I will now integrate the data into the user interface.\n\n\n\n**Finalizing the Database Integration**\n\nI\'m now completing the integration with the user interface by adapting `getTransactions()`. I\'ve completed all of the MongoDB data type conversions, along with the fallback logic. I have ensured data consistency and correct formatting of transactions for a seamless user experience. I am now prepared to test.\n\n\n"
