  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Onboarding Flows**\n\nI\'m focusing now on the onboarding flow itself. Specifically, I\'m thinking about the user experience. The initial thought of sending an OTP code at signup remains, but I\'m refining it further. The expectation is that the user verifies immediately during sign-up, not later, so I\'m shifting to how that works in the context of our pending list.\n\n\n**Refining Sign-Up Flow**\n\nOkay, I\'m now zeroing in on a critical adjustment to the sign-up sequence. Currently, if someone needs approval, the system skips OTP verification and directly goes to a pending state. I\'m proposing a change to ensure an OTP code is ALWAYS sent during sign-up. The idea is to save new sign-ups as pending, which triggers an OTP request and takes them to verification step, even if they need approval. This aligns better with the user\'s expectation of immediate identity verification. I believe this solves the user\'s issue with verification.\n\n\n**Updating OTP Logic**\n\nI\'m now focused on the mechanics of sending and verifying OTPs for pending approvals. I\'ve re-examined `OnboardingView.tsx` and identified the direct skip to the pending checklist. I think that instead of skipping OTP, we can change the logic. The current approach bypasses verification for pending users. I believe we can now set the pending ID and trigger the OTP, even if approval is needed. The aim is to make the `/api/auth/send-otp` and `/api/auth/verify-otp` endpoints compatible with pending IDs.\n\n\n**Confirming OTP Verification**\n\nI\'m now confirming the implications of a successful OTP verification. The user\'s contact information is verified upon entering the correct 6-digit code. I\'m focusing on the `OnboardingView.tsx` where a successful verification triggers a state change and navigates the user. Inside `AppContext.tsx`, I see the `verifyOtp` function uses a POST request to `/api/auth/verify-otp`.\n\n\n**Clarifying Login Logic**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the post-OTP verification flow for pending signups. I\'ve realized that a pending signup shouldn\'t immediately log the user in. Instead, I\'m proposing a display of a \"pending checklist\" screen or notification. The successful verification of the OTP means the user\'s phone number is confirmed, but login needs approval from Charles. This aligns with user expectations and fulfills the user request.\n\n\n**Finalizing the Signup Flow**\n\nI\'ve got it! The revised sign-up process now mandates OTP verification, even for pending approvals. A user completes steps to sign up, receives a verification code, and enters it to verify their number. Upon successful verification, the flow transitions to a pending approval screen, confirming their information is verified and awaits Charles\'s authorization. This approach elegantly integrates verification with the pending status and fully addresses the user\342\200\231s request.\n\n\n**Implementing OTP in Pending**\n\nI\'m now diving into the database schema and functions to support OTP verification for pending signups. I\'m focusing on adding `otp`, `otpExpiry`, and `otpMethod` fields to the `PendingSignup` interface in `src/db.ts`. I also need to make sure I create the relevant `updatePendingSignup` function to correctly handle these fields. My current focus is implementing the schema updates correctly to ensure that the OTP logic works as intended.\n\n\n**Adding Pending Signup Fields**\n\nOkay, I\'ve got a much better understanding of the schema now. Specifically, the `pendingSignupSchema` in `src/db.ts` currently does not have `otp`, `otpExpiry`, or `otpMethod` fields. My plan is to update the mongoose schema to include these fields so that we can have a full implementation for the OTP process for pending signups. I\'m also going to add a new `updatePendingSignup` function in `src/db.ts`. I believe that will complete my understanding of the schema and will greatly improve the implementation.\n\n\n**Reviewing Database Updates**\n\nI\'m now reviewing changes to `src/db.ts`, specifically focusing on how the OTP fields are incorporated into database queries. I\'m carefully examining the `findPendingSignupById`, `findPendingSignupByEmail`, and `createPendingSignup` functions. I\'m confirming that the new `otp`, `otpExpiry`, and `otpMethod` fields are correctly included in the schema and associated queries. I\'m also ensuring the data retrieval and creation logic is fully updated.\n\n\n**Implementing `updatePendingSignup`**\n\nI\'m implementing an `updatePendingSignup` function now, incorporating both MongoDB and a local fallback for future compatibility. The MongoDB version converts `otpExpiry` to a Date object. The local fallback method supports partial updates using a find-and-update approach. I am ensuring that the correct data fields are present in the function.\n\n\n**Verifying Function Integration**\n\nI\'m now confirming the integration of the `updatePendingSignup` function in `server.ts`. I have examined the import statements and confirmed that the function has been imported and is ready for use, alongside other relevant database functions. I am now confident about the flow of information for the OTP feature.\n\n\n**Adapting OTP Route**\n\nI\'m now modifying the `/api/auth/send-otp` route to handle both existing users and pending signups. I am updating the current logic to check for the user first, then verify the pending user by ID if the initial check fails. I\'ll need to update the endpoint to use this new logic, and provide the correct response.\n\n\n**Refining Pending Logic**\n\nI\'m now refining the logic within the `/api/auth/send-otp` route to streamline the pending signup process. The integration I did ensures OTP is sent to pending signups. I\'m focusing on incorporating the `isPending` flag to ensure smooth transitions. I believe the new logic is cleaner.\n\n\n"
